<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António Vieira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1608">1608</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António Vieira (1608–1697) was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary, diplomat and preacher, considered a master of classical Portuguese prose. Raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> IV as an adviser and envoy after 1640, and from 1653 led the Jesuit missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> and Grão-Pará, where he campaigned against the enslavement of indigenous people until the settlers expelled the Jesuits in 1661. He was later tried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-vieira.jpg" alt="António Vieira" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padre_Ant%C3%B3nio_Vieira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
